{"attack": true, "case": "The target claims the Popperian preference for well-tested theories avoids induction by reframing it as a procedural rule that 'maximizes the objective chance of learning from future errors' and 'ensures that if error exists, it is found quickly.' But this justification is itself inductive: asserting that more severe tests will yield faster error detection requires an expectation about how nature will respond to future testing — exactly the kind of forward-looking assumption the claim disclaims. The target explicitly concedes (countercondition 3) that invoking minimization of practical-failure risk smuggles in induction about nature's uniformity, yet 'maximizing the speed of theoretical correction' relies on the identical assumption: that the world will cooperate with severe tests by revealing errors promptly. The word 'ensures' in the mechanism is a guarantee about future outcomes, contradicting the claim's own admission that 'this costs us any guarantee of practical success.' The distinction between 'practical failure' and 'theoretical correction' is therefore illusory — both are future epistemic outcomes, and preferring one rule over another on the basis of expected future learning is the inductive inference the target purports to eliminate.", "counterexample": ["A theory that has survived severe tests could still contain errors that only manifest under conditions no future test will reach, in which case 'ensuring error is found quickly' fails regardless of test severity — the 'maximization' claim silently assumes nature will present the relevant conditions, an inductive premise."], "premise": "Claiming that a procedural rule 'maximizes the objective chance of learning from future errors' or 'ensures that if error exists, it is found quickly' is a forward-looking expectation about outcomes, not a mere procedural description, and therefore requires inductive assumptions about how nature responds to testing.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "0c8f46c20335c9872d1862c6246566dd2eec17fa4bf66b08e5a771aa573eea24", "quote": "thereby maximizing the objective chance of learning from future errors; accepting this costs us any guarantee of practical success or proximity to truth, reducing scientific action to a gamble"}, {"block": "0c8f46c20335c9872d1862c6246566dd2eec17fa4bf66b08e5a771aa573eea24", "quote": "ensuring that if error exists, it is found quickly. This converts the 'smuggled induction' into an explicit commitment to rapid error elimination."}], "successor_question": "Can a non-inductive procedural rule be justified over alternatives without any claim about the expected epistemic outcomes of following it, and if not, does Popper's conventionalist move inevitably smuggle in the very induction it seeks to avoid?"}